            "items": [
              {
                "arxiv_id": "2611.12564v1",
                "title": "GHiML: VLM-Guided Hierarchical Multi-Label Learning with Applications to Benthic Imagery",
                "summary": "Vision language models (VLMs) encode substantial expert knowledge within their weights, yet their direct application to specialised classification domains such as benthic imagery remains limited. Meanwhile, vision transformers trained on these tasks rely predominantly on low-level visual latents (colours, textures, and shapes) that lack the higher-level reasoning humans employ for identification. We propose Guided Hierarchical Multi-Label (GHiML) learning, a pipeline that extracts conceptual latent variables from a VLM conditioned on training data, generates sample-relevant captions grounded in those variables, and fuses the resulting textual embeddings with image embeddings via cross-attention and a per-node gated router. We also introduce the inverted HML score ($d_{\\mathrm{inv}}$), a similarity score that evaluates hierarchical multi-label predictions through graph traversal distance and a cardinality penalty, providing an interpretable alternative to existing measures, which are insensitive to realistic degradations in sparse, deep hierarchies. Experiments on BenthicNet, FathomNet and iNaturalist show that the router improves macro $F_1$ over the vision-only baseline by up to 6.7 pp and $d_{\\mathrm{inv}}$ by up to 21.1 pp on in-domain benthic data, with gains persisting under distribution shift. Ablations locate the contribution of the text itself: on the multi-label biotic hierarchies, removing the per-sample text signal degrades $d_{\\mathrm{inv}}$ while leaving macro $F_1$ unchanged, consistent with text acting through prediction cardinality rather than node discrimination, whereas iNaturalist, whose annotations are single-label, loses 18.7% of macro $F_1$. Our results support the hypothesis that VLMs contain exploitable specialised knowledge that, when appropriately elicited, can augment classification in domains where direct visual learning is insufficient.",
                "authors": [
                  "Isaac Xu",
                  "Devi Ayyagari",
                  "Martin Gillis",
                  "Benjamin Misiuk",
                  "Craig J. Brown",
                  "Thomas Trappenberg"
                ],
                "published": "2026-11-09T15:28:27Z",
                "updated": "2026-11-09T15:28:27Z",
                "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12564v1",
                "pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.12564v1",
                "source": "arxiv.org",
                "primary_category": "cs.CV",
                "categories": [
                  "cs.CV"
                ]
              },
              {
                "arxiv_id": "2511.19208v4",
                "title": "Leader Election via Unique Sink Orientation",
                "summary": "A proof-labeling scheme (PLS) certifies a global property using only local checks. That is, a prover assigns a certificate to every node and each node accepts or rejects by inspecting only the certificates in its neighborhood, so that every graph satisfying some global property admits a certificate assignment accepted by all nodes (existence) and every certificate assignment accepted by all nodes gives a legal configuration (soundness). In this work, we design PLSs for two classical problems, leader certification and spanning tree certification, assuming that all nodes are anonymous, for a specific class of graphs. Certifying a unique leader is impossible in general anonymous graphs for any certificate size due to a well-known result for rings by [Korman, Kutten and Peleg, 2010]. This raises the question of which graph classes admit a PLS for leader certification with anonymous nodes, ideally with size independent of the size of the graph. Our work contributes to a growing list of such classes, which includes chordal graphs, meshed graphs and simply connected subsets of the triangular grid. We consider dismantlable graphs, a class which contains trees, chordal, bridged and Helly graphs, as well as the $k$-th powers of $δ$-hyperbolic graphs for $k \\geq 2δ$. To the best of our knowledge, these are the first PLSs tailored to dismantlable graphs, potentially highlighting structural properties useful for certifying additional problems. Finally, we present a generic transformation that converts any labeling scheme with certificates of finite type into a silent self-stabilizing algorithm by adding only one extra state, assuming a Gouda fair scheduler, whereas known generic transformations require $O(\\ell + \\log n)$ bits for $\\ell$-bit certificates. This transformation may be of independent interest.",
                "authors": [
                  "Jérémie Chalopin",
                  "Maria Kokkou"
                ],
                "published": "2025-11-24T15:17:32Z",
                "updated": "2026-11-09T15:23:35Z",
                "url": "http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.19208v4",
                "pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.19208v4",
                "source": "arxiv.org",
                "primary_category": "cs.DC",
                "categories": [
                  "cs.DC"
                ]
              },
              {
                "arxiv_id": "2610.19730v2",
                "title": "Design and Analysis of Temporal Representations in Neural Networks",
                "summary": "Neural networks, spiking or not, compute with amplitudes: activation values, spike counts, firing rates, never event times. No measure exists of how much a network uses time. We propose one, the degree of temporal representation: the fraction of a network's four functions (encoding, activation, decoding, learning) whose variables or parameters are temporal, and hypothesise that it predicts performance on temporal tasks. At fixed architecture, accuracy and energy efficiency increase with the degree: a delay network of maximal degree reaches 98.94\\% on Google Speech Commands with 175k parameters, above transformers. To test whether networks of different degree really differ, we convert the delay network into artificial and spiking networks with the same inputs and outputs. From this common start, the artificial one learns differently and temporal gradients depend on event times. Hence we obtain a design rule: for temporal data, make event times variables of a network's functions. Spiking networks, converted after training, keep the delay network's accuracy, unreachable by spiking networks trained from scratch.",
